  "market_context": "Uptrend on GER40 1-hour chart; price made a higher high near 18682.9 while RSI made a lower high, forming a regular bearish divergence. One RSI peak was above 70 (overbought), satisfying the 70/30 filter.",
  "key_observation": "Price formed a higher high at ~18682.9 while RSI formed a lower high, confirming a regular bearish divergence; the RSI peak was overbought (>70), increasing reversal probability per the analyst’s filter.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identified an uptrend on the GER40 1-hour chart with consecutive higher highs and higher lows.",
    "Step 2: Located two swing highs in price (higher high at ~18682.9 and prior high), then compared corresponding RSI peaks.",
    "Step 3: Observed that RSI made a lower high while price made a higher high, confirming a regular bearish divergence.",
    "Step 4: Verified that one of the RSI peaks was above the 70 level (overbought), satisfying the analyst’s 70/30 filter for higher-probability divergences.",
    "Step 5: Noted that price subsequently spiked down but then resumed the prior uptrend, illustrating the caveat that not all regular divergences lead to full reversals."
  ],
  "outcome": "Price spiked down after the divergence but continued the prior uptrend — no sustained reversal occurred.",
  "lessons": "Regular bearish divergences require confluence (e.g., RSI >70) to increase reliability; they signal weakening momentum but do not guarantee reversal—trend continuation is common, especially without structural break.",
